!type: Basic Math
!deck: Math::Real-Numbers
!tags: Math Real-Numbers Absolute-Value Inequality

!id: C3mD0XhGrY
!front: Define the absolute value \( |x| \) of a real number \( x \).
!back:
\[
|x| =
\begin{cases}
    x, & x \ge 0, \\
    -x, & x < 0.
\end{cases}
\]

So \( |x| \) is the nonnegative magnitude of \( x \), ignoring its sign.
~~~

!id: OKudtzzxwG
!front: What are the basic nonnegativity and zero properties of \( |x| \) for \( x \in \mathbb{R} \)?
!back:
For every \( x \in \mathbb{R} \):

- \( |x| \ge 0 \).
- \( |x| = 0 \) if and only if \( x = 0 \).
~~~

!id: bkDNDuoT9y
!front: Why does \( |-x| = |x| \) for every real number \( x \)?
!back:
Absolute value ignores sign. More formally:

- If \( x \ge 0 \), then \( |x|=x \) and \( |-x|=-(-x)=x \).
- If \( x < 0 \), then \( |x|=-x \) and \( |-x|=-x \), since \( -x>0 \).

So \( |-x|=|x| \) in both cases.
~~~

!id: bSkEfDn71X
!front: How is \( |x| \) related to \( x^2 \)?
!back:
For every real number \( x \), \[ |x|^2 = x^2. \]

Equivalently, \[ |x| = \sqrt{x^2}, \] where \( \sqrt{x^2} \) denotes the nonnegative square root of \( x^2 \).
~~~

!id: SOkq1mTQZD
!front: Interpret \( |x-a| \) geometrically on the real line.
!back:
\( |x-a| \) is the distance between the real numbers \( x \) and \( a \) on the real line.

It is symmetric: \[ |x-a| = |a-x|. \]
~~~

!id: ZeXr2wL93j
!front: Convert \( |x-a| < r \), with \( r > 0 \), into a double inequality.
!back:
\[
    |x-a| < r
    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
    -r < x-a < r
    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
    a-r < x < a+r.
\]

So \( |x-a| < r \) means \( x \) lies inside the open interval centered at \( a \) with radius \( r \).
~~~

!id: YVpH3sppeh
!front: Convert \( |x-a| \le r \), with \( r \ge 0 \), into a double inequality.
!back:
\[
    |x-a| \le r
    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
    -r \le x-a \le r
    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
    a-r \le x \le a+r.
\]

So \( |x-a| \le r \) means \( x \) lies inside the closed interval centered at \( a \) with radius \( r \).
~~~

!id: QUQnqVj8gf
!front: Convert \( |x-a| > r \), with \( r \ge 0 \), into ordinary inequalities.
!back:
\[
    |x-a| > r
    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
    x-a > r \text{ or } x-a < -r.
\]

Equivalently, \[ x > a+r \quad\text{or}\quad x < a-r. \]

So \( x \) lies outside the closed interval \( [a-r,a+r] \).
~~~

!id: 8keu7a5h4b
!front: Why does \( |x| \le M \), with \( M \ge 0 \), imply both an upper and a lower bound for \( x \)?
!back:
\[
    |x| \le M
    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
    -M \le x \le M.
\]

Thus \( M \) is an upper bound for \( x \), and \( -M \) is a lower bound for \( x \).
!extra:
The assumption \( M \ge 0 \) matters because \( |x| \) is always nonnegative.
If \( M < 0 \), no real \( x \) can satisfy \( |x| \le M \).
~~~

!id: lbGh7NjyEO
!front: Why does \( |x| < M \), with \( M > 0 \), mean \( x \) is trapped between \( -M \) and \( M \)?
!back:
\[
    |x| < M
    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
    -M < x < M.
\]

This says \( x \) is within distance \( M \) of \( 0 \).
~~~

!id: 9BwYi1Dtcm
!front: Characterize bounded subsets of \( \mathbb{R} \) using absolute value.
!back: A set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) is bounded if and only if there exists \( M \ge 0 \) such that \[ |a| \le M \] for every \( a \in A \).
!extra: This single inequality combines an upper bound \( a \le M \) and a lower bound \( a \ge -M \).
~~~

!id: OYERfFZfqi
!front: State the triangle inequality for real numbers.
!back:
For all real numbers \( x \) and \( y \), \[ |x+y| \le |x|+|y|. \]

It says the magnitude of a sum is at most the sum of the magnitudes.
~~~

!id: Y7atRaR4qL
!front: State the reverse triangle inequality for real numbers.
!back:
For all real numbers \( x \) and \( y \), \[ \bigl||x|-|y|\bigr| \le |x-y|. \]

It says the distance between magnitudes is at most the distance between the original numbers.
~~~

!id: Xfru4ruT3x
!front: How does the triangle inequality estimate the distance from \( x \) to \( y \) through an intermediate point \( a \)?
!back:
For all real numbers \( x,a,y \), \[ |x-y| = |(x-a)+(a-y)| \le |x-a|+|a-y|. \]

So the direct distance from \( x \) to \( y \) is at most the distance from \( x \) to \( a \) plus the distance from \( a \) to \( y \).
~~~

!id: Gn7jJlym99
!front: How does absolute value behave with products of real numbers?
!back:
For all real numbers \( x \) and \( y \), \[ |xy| = |x|\,|y|. \]

This lets bounds multiply cleanly: if \( |x| \le M \) and \( |y| \le N \), with \( M,N \ge 0 \), then \[ |xy| \le MN. \]
~~~

!id: Dx9eDWhbrK
!front: How does absolute value behave with reciprocals and quotients of real numbers?
!back:
If \( x \ne 0 \), then \[ \left|\frac{1}{x}\right| = \frac{1}{|x|}. \]

If \( y \ne 0 \), then \[ \left|\frac{x}{y}\right| = \frac{|x|}{|y|}. \]
~~~

!id: P9KMLP4z9B
!front: If \( |x-a| < \delta \) and \( |y-b| < \varepsilon \), how can you bound \( |(x+y)-(a+b)| \)?
!back:
Use the triangle inequality:
\[
\begin{align*}
    |(x+y)-(a+b)|
        &= |(x-a)+(y-b)| \\
        &\le |x-a|+|y-b| \\
        &< \delta+\varepsilon.
\end{align*}
\]
This is the basic estimate behind proving that sums preserve closeness.
~~~

!id: RZhId0Cdoh
!front: If \( |x| \le M \) and \( |x-y| < \varepsilon \), how can you bound \( |y| \)?
!back:
Use \( y=x+(y-x) \) and the triangle inequality: \[ |y| \le |x|+|y-x| < M+\varepsilon. \]

So a number close to a bounded number is also bounded, with a slightly larger bound.
~~~
